1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:11,725
On YouTube, Riley Coleman films himself growing a tiny futures account, and says the
point is "showing you what's possible if you follow a disciplined, consistent strategy".

2
00:00:12,233 --> 00:00:16,921
If discipline is what it takes, how many of
the people who try actually end up ahead?

3
00:00:17,433 --> 00:00:22,383
Researchers in Brazil and Taiwan counted,
and they counted the winners too.

4
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:32,050
Ross Cameron of Warrior Trading tells beginners to "learn a strategy that is proven
profitable by someone else even if there's no guarantee it'll work for you".

5
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:41,792
Another channel, RP Profits, is blunter: "The strategy is real. The
math is real, and there's people using it right now to make money."

6
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:49,037
Yet the Warrior Trading blog itself carries the line
"Trading is risky, and most day traders lose money."

7
00:00:50,067 --> 00:01:01,229
The Brazilian study followed people who began day trading in the country's equity
futures market between 2013 and 2015, and sorted them by how many days they kept at it.

8
00:01:01,233 --> 00:01:09,483
Of those who tried it for a single day, 29.8 percent made
a net profit, and the share fell at every step after that.

9
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:14,825
The authors compare it to roulette, where the more
rounds people play, the fewer come out ahead.

10
00:01:14,833 --> 00:01:25,221
Of the 1,551 who kept going for more than 300 days, 97
percent lost money after fees, and only 47 came out ahead.

11
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:33,533
Only 17 earned more than Brazil's minimum wage,
and the single best averaged 310 dollars a day.

12
00:01:33,533 --> 00:01:42,621
And the handful who beat a bank teller's starting pay got there on a roller
coaster, their daily results swinging by hundreds to thousands of dollars.

13
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:45,683
Did the ones who stayed at
least get better with practice?

14
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,713
The study found no evidence
that they learned.

15
00:01:49,733 --> 00:02:00,283
A second study, of individual investors who day traded in Taiwan
from 1992 to 2006, found the vast majority of them unprofitable.

16
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:11,638
Taken as a group, they lost 7 basis points a day before costs, where a basis point
is a hundredth of a percent, and trading costs more than tripled that loss, to 23.9.

17
00:02:11,667 --> 00:02:14,179
So is anyone reliably good at this?

18
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:24,612
A few are: in Taiwan, traders who had already been profitable, with more than 40
days of day trading in the past year, went on to earn more than their trading costs.

19
00:02:24,633 --> 00:02:31,983
But those predictably profitable traders made up less
than 3 percent of all day traders on an average day.

20
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:43,013
Ross Cameron names one reason the rest struggle: "when you trade against the high
frequency trading algorithms, trading large caps, you really don't have a good edge".

21
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:54,671
The Brazilian futures data point the same way in a different market: when high-frequency
traders' share of deals rose by 10 percent, individuals' average loss was 19 percent higher.

22
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:03,175
So yes: in both studies most people lost, and in Brazil the
longer they kept at it, the worse their odds of being ahead.

23
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:12,038
Some people do beat the odds, and in Taiwan the ones who had been winning
tended to keep winning, but they were a sliver of everyone who tried.

24
00:03:12,067 --> 00:03:20,879
Both studies looked at other markets and other years than today's American
stock market, so they give the odds there, not a law for every market.

25
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:32,112
What these counts can't do is check any one trader's month: a screenshot of a small account
turned into a big one is neither confirmed nor ruled out by a tally of everyone who tried.

26
00:03:32,633 --> 00:03:41,121
If someone sells you a strategy as consistent, ask for audited results
across every account and every month, not the best screenshot.

27
00:03:41,433 --> 00:03:49,471
And if you try it anyway, put in only what you can afford to lose,
and judge yourself by your results after fees, not by your best day.
